afk: the apply=false capture note names a branch->patch downgrade
loudly instead of rendering it as a normal patch capture.

Motivation: when task.isolation.merge is pinned to `branch` and
commitToBranch throws (renamed from commitToTaskRef in 18.x), isolation-runner falls back to writing a
`<id>.patch` artifact and sets result.error = "Merge failed: ...".
Under apply: false, structured-subagent's summary then read
"Isolation: changes captured at `<id>.patch` (apply=false). Not
applied." — byte-identical to a configured patch-mode run, and
result.error is never rendered. That is how 97 downgraded captures
accumulated unnoticed over three days while
`git for-each-ref refs/omp/task` read clean (see
backlog/2026-08-10-isolation-branch-merge-falls-back-to-patch.md in
afk; the downgrade cause itself was fixed by the 17.2.12 bump).

What must survive a rebase:
- In the apply=false summary branch: mergeMode === "branch" plus a
  patchPath (no branchName) renders a <system-notification> naming the
  FAILED branch capture, including result.error, the patch path, and
  the instruction that the patch is NOT on a task ref.
- mergeMode === "branch" with an error and NO artifact at all gets its
  own loud notification (changes lost) instead of "no changes
  captured."
- Plain patch-mode captures and genuine no-change runs keep their
  original quiet wording.

diff --git a/packages/coding-agent/src/task/structured-subagent.ts b/packages/coding-agent/src/task/structured-subagent.ts
index a3b6197..4af8fa8 100644
--- a/packages/coding-agent/src/task/structured-subagent.ts
+++ b/packages/coding-agent/src/task/structured-subagent.ts
@@ -665,10 +665,19 @@ export async function runStructuredSubagent(request: StructuredSubagentRequest):
 		} else if (policy.isIsolated && isolationContext && !policy.applyChanges) {
 			if (result.branchName)
 				mergeSummary = `\n\nIsolation: changes captured on branch \`${result.branchName}\` (apply=false). Not merged.`;
+			else if (result.patchPath && policy.mergeMode === "branch")
+				// A configured branch capture that produced a patch artifact is a
+				// DOWNGRADE (commitToBranch threw; isolation-runner fell back to a
+				// patch). Without result.error in the note the summary is
+				// indistinguishable from a configured patch-mode run — which is how
+				// 97 downgraded captures went unnoticed for three days.
+				mergeSummary = `\n\n<system-notification>Isolation: configured branch capture FAILED${result.error ? ` (${result.error})` : ""}; changes fell back to a patch artifact at \`${result.patchPath}\` (apply=false). Not applied. Review the patch by hand — it is not on a task ref.</system-notification>`;
 			else if (result.patchPath)
 				mergeSummary = `\n\nIsolation: changes captured at \`${result.patchPath}\` (apply=false). Not applied.`;
 			else if ((result.nestedPatches?.length ?? 0) > 0)
 				mergeSummary = `\n\nIsolation: changes captured for ${result.nestedPatches?.length} nested ${(result.nestedPatches?.length ?? 0) === 1 ? "repository" : "repositories"} (apply=false). Not applied.`;
+			else if (result.error && policy.mergeMode === "branch")
+				mergeSummary = `\n\n<system-notification>Isolation: configured branch capture FAILED (${result.error}) and no patch artifact could be written. The subagent's changes were lost.</system-notification>`;
 			else mergeSummary = "\n\nIsolation: no changes captured.";
 		}
 
